WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.950
Hi, I'm Alex
Cookson and I'd like

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:11.880
to talk about the power
of great datasets.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.320
I'd like to start off this
talk by posing a question

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.240
that I think all of us have
asked ourselves at one point

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:21.710
and that is, how do I learn
to do this thing in R?

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:24.380
Whether that's sentiment
analysis, or data

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:28.990
visualization, or web scraping,
how do we learn something?

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:32.439
And Allison Horst who does a
lot of wonderful illustrations

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:36.760
about R and statistics has one
that answers this question.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:40.900
And it shows how teachers,
mentors, and the wonderful R

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.900
stats community all
contribute to taking someone

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:46.810
from being a beginner to
some level of proficiency

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:48.440
with something.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:50.900
But I always look
at this illustration

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:57.413
and ask myself, what
about great datasets?

00:00:57.413 --> 00:00:58.830
Now when I say
great datasets, I'm

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:00.970
talking about something
pretty specific.

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:05.260
I'm talking about datasets that
are both cool and interesting.

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:07.577
And when I say cool,
I mean something

00:01:07.577 --> 00:01:09.910
that when you come across
that you say to yourself, hey,

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:12.040
that's cool or
that's really fun.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.790
It's something that speaks
to you on a deeper level

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:16.920
and it tends to be
pretty personal.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:20.230
So what's cool to you
might not be cool to me.

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:22.050
And when I say
interesting, I mean

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:23.940
that it rouses your curiosity.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.520
It makes you start asking
a whole bunch of questions

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.857
that you really
want the answer to.

00:01:28.857 --> 00:01:31.440
If you ever played a video game
where you say to yourself just

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.750
five more minutes
or one more turn

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:35.880
and you're still playing
it an hour later,

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:40.410
this is the dataset version
of that, just one more graph.

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:45.600
So some examples that I enjoy
are the Duke Lemur Center

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.720
dataset which looks at
ages and weights of almost

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.940
30 different species of Lemur.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:52.770
The Broadway Grosses
dataset which

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:58.920
looks at weekly box office
grosses, tickets sold,

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:01.170
average top ticket
prices of Broadway

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.810
shows going back to 1985.

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:05.520
And fictional
character personalities

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.350
which I consider to
be one of the all

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:10.479
time greatest of great datasets.

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:12.660
And this one looks
at personality traits

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:15.750
of 800 fictional
characters across over 250

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:19.680
different spectrums, such as the
playful to serious spectrum,

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.930
where we might find Michael
Scott from The Office

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:24.690
on the playful side,
and Worf from Star Trek:

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:28.453
Next Generation way over
on the serious side.

00:02:28.453 --> 00:02:30.120
And I think this is
such a great dataset

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:33.257
because I've been watching
so much TV and so many movies

00:02:33.257 --> 00:02:35.340
lately that when I came
across that I thought, Oh,

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:36.460
this is so fun.

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:38.540
It's something that
speaks to, something

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:40.290
I've been spending a
lot of my time doing.

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:42.780
And I immediately had
tons and tons of questions

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:46.980
that I wanted to ask it and
uncover from this dataset.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.170
One of those questions
was, what are

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:51.743
some of my favorite
characters personalities?

00:02:51.743 --> 00:02:53.160
And I put in a
whole bunch of work

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:54.577
and ended up with
some graphs that

00:02:54.577 --> 00:02:58.410
looked like this which takes
the six characters from Pride

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:01.570
and Prejudice and shows
their strongest traits.

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:04.110
So we see that Elizabeth
Bennet at the top left

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:06.380
is a treasure as
opposed to trash.

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:08.760
She's important,
she has a high IQ,

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.120
she's independent, and
beautiful, and a feminist.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:14.760
But to put this
together, I had to learn

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:18.150
to do a whole bunch of things
I didn't know how to do before

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:21.780
like use custom fonts so I
could get this Georgian era

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:24.930
kind of handwriting
vibe, to tweak labels

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:27.200
using the glue and
ggtext packages,

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:29.700
I had to choose an appropriate
color palette for the subject

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:34.920
matter, and adjust a ton of
theme elements using ggplot2.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:37.440
A second question I had
was, which characters

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:40.360
are most similar to or
different from one another?

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:41.850
And again I put
in a bunch of work

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:43.630
and ended up with
something like this,

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:46.320
which looks at reasonable
versus derange characters,

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:49.780
plotted against rugged
versus refined characters.

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:52.740
So here we see that characters
like Sandor Clegane and Jayne

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:54.570
Cobb are really
similar to one another.

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:56.040
They're deranged and rugged.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.620
And their opposites are
Charlotte York from Sex

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:02.700
and the City and Annie Edison,
who are reasonable and refined.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:06.300
And to do this, I had to conduct
principal component analysis

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:08.760
with tidymodels because
these are actually

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.880
principal components, you
don't find them in the data.

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:14.280
I had to learn to add
text and arrow annotations

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:17.399
to get this graph here and
I wrote a blog post where

00:04:17.399 --> 00:04:19.079
this is an interactive chart.

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:23.400
So I had to learn how to make an
interactive chart using plotly.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:26.040
And if I can be honest
with you for a moment,

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:29.430
I never would have
put this much effort.

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:33.100
This many hours into
analyzing Titanic passengers.

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:36.450
It was because it was such
a great, engaging dataset

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:38.340
where I had tons of
questions that I wanted

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:41.520
the answers to that I had
the drive and the curiosity

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:43.740
to actually get those answers
and learn a whole bunch

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:45.430
of stuff in the process.

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:47.850
So I encourage you to get
your own great datasets.

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:51.150
And to Allison, I
very respectfully

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:54.490
think we may add one
thing to the balloon.

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:56.340
Thank you.

